"""patch_prereg_addendum10_1030.py (2026-10-30): appends Addendum 10 (the rebuild procedure for event_premium_20d) to results/PREREG_g7_proven_rerun_2026-10-29.md.
The registration must be exactly the version after Addendum 9 (sha256 8b372b8c...) and the placeholder must occur exactly once, or nothing is changed.
No registered prediction is edited: the text before PRED-TABLE-END is compared before and after.
Usage: python3 scripts/audit/patch_prereg_addendum10_1030.py [results/PREREG_g7_proven_rerun_2026-10-29.md]"""
import hashlib, sys
def sha(s): return hashlib.sha256((s if s.endswith("\n") else s + "\n").encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()
p = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else "results/PREREG_g7_proven_rerun_2026-10-29.md"
T = open(p, encoding="utf-8").read()
assert sha(T) == "8b372b8c755393b4ecea8d0bc1d045a1f33b9cd12619000ec7f6519602b305c5", "the registration is not the version after Addendum 9; nothing changed"
old = "(further dated addenda go here: the remaining procedures)"
assert T.count(old) == 1, "placeholder not found exactly once; nothing changed"
new = """### Addendum 10 (2026-10-30, before the build): event_premium_20d, a rebuild into new tables

**What the flag measures, from the ledger and the database.** For events of the ordinary types (everything except capital_raise and corporate_action), the 20-day absolute abnormal return scaled by realized volatility, |abnormal_return_20d| / (rv90 * sqrt(20)), is compared with a baseline matched on the calendar month and on the volatility ratio v64 / v250 just before the event (four bins: below 0.775, 0.775 to 0.950, 0.950 to 1.162, and 1.162 or more; the table mm_matched holds one baseline per month and bin). The premium is the mean of the scaled move minus the matched baseline. The ledger row (status proven, tested 2026-10-16, multiplier 1.05, repaired 2026-10-19) carries this rule: 2004-2018 pooled, an interval that contains +0.0295 is consistent; positive with z above 2 but excluding it is replicated lower; an upper end below +0.02 is not replicated. The stored result, recomputed on 2026-10-30 from events_ratio with the comparison query recovered from the 2026-10-20 session, equals the ledger's repaired evidence: 2004-07 +0.0292 (n 2,626), 2008-12 +0.0490 (3,225), 2013-18 +0.0794 (3,561), 2019-26 older +0.0367 (4,371), wave 1 +0.0877 (499), wave 2 +0.1243 (310), and pooled 2004-2018 +0.0550 (SE 0.0091, n 9,412); 14,592 ordinary rows in all.

**Why a rebuild and not a re-run.** The function ratio_period(lo, hi) inserts only the events that are missing from events_ratio, so stored values are never refreshed, and the table was repaired in place on 2026-10-19; calling the function again adds nothing. The rebuild copies the live function body, read from the database on 2026-10-30 (identical to the text of the 2026-10-16 session; checked clause for clause by a whitespace-insensitive comparison), into two new empty tables: ARM 0 is the original procedure on today's inputs; ARM 1 is the same with events that have an exclude row in event_exclusions left out. The two files differ from the live function only in the names of the target table and function and, in ARM 1, one added clause.

**The registered rule has a hole, and the scoring is fixed here, before any run.** The ledger notes: the out-of-time rule had no category for a result above +0.0295. The repaired pooled estimate is above it. The category of the pooled 2004-2018 premium of the older cohort (ordinary types, naive SE, z = estimate over SE) is: A consistent, if estimate plus or minus 2 SE contains +0.0295; B above the registered range, if the estimate is positive, z is above 2 and the interval lies wholly above +0.0295 (the rule's second clause, positive with z above 2 excluding +0.0295, is met by its letter, but its name says lower); C replicated lower, if positive, z above 2 and the interval lies wholly below +0.0295; D not replicated, if the upper end is below +0.02. The registered p35 (still passes its registered rule) is scored 1 for A, B or C and 0 for D. The registered p36 (keeps the same rule category) is scored 1 if the ARM 1 category equals the category of the stored result today, which is B (estimate +0.0550, SE 0.0091, interval 0.0368 to 0.0732), and 0 otherwise; the category is also reported against the pre-repair evidence of 2026-10-16 (the original era rows of the ledger, n-weighted: +0.0470, SE 0.0091, interval 0.0289 to 0.0651, category A by a margin of 0.0006), so that a reader who takes that as the reference can see the other reading. The registered p33 and p34 describe ARM 1: the pooled 2004-2018 premium and the 2019-2026 older-events premium.

**Every input, checked on 2026-10-30 in the database (the rule of Addendum 4a).** The function body (live definition); tables events (title, event_date), event_realized_vol (19,795 rows, rv90), securities, event_type_relationships and event_types, event_market_reactions_corrected (22,486 rows), reactions_view_only (a table, 4,971 rows, repaired on 2026-10-19 from the frozen table event_market_reactions_v2_snapshot_20261019), vol_estimators (latest day 2026-09-15), mm_matched (1,220 rows, last month 2026-07-01), new_events_20261015 (2,106 rows) and new_events_wave2 (1,556 rows) for the cohorts, and event_exclusions for ARM 1; the view event_component_dates (4,180 rows), which is built from events and candidate_review_log rows whose verdict is real_event, and candidate_review_log was touched by the 2026-10-29 review, so a rebuild can exclude different events than the stored table did. events_ratio (16,993 rows; 14,592 ordinary; months 2004-01-01 to 2026-07-01) is read for comparison only and is not modified; 6 of its rows belong to excluded events.

**The build (steps in the SQL editor; the statements are the files committed with this addendum).** (1) scripts/sql/g7_premium_build_arm0_2026-10-30.sql creates events_ratio_g7_arm0 and the function ratio_period_g7_arm0; the same for ARM 1 with the arm1 file. (2) ARM 0 is filled with three calls, as the original session did: 2004-01-01 to 2011-01-01, 2011-01-01 to 2019-01-01, 2019-01-01 to 2026-10-01; ARM 1 the same. (3) QUERY 1 of scripts/sql/g7_premium_compare_2026-10-30.sql compares the stored table with ARM 0 (rows in both, only in one, common rows that differ) and ARM 0 with ARM 1. (4) QUERY 2 prints the matched excess by era and cohort for the stored table, ARM 0 and ARM 1, with the naive SE and an SE clustered by month.

**Tripwires.** (1) Each call prints the number of rows inserted; the three ARM 0 counts are added up and compared with the 16,993 stored rows (all buckets). (2) ARM 1 must have fewer rows than ARM 0 by at least 1 and at most the number of ARM 0 rows that have an exclude row (QUERY 1). (3) The stored table is not modified: QUERY 2 re-reads it (its pooled 2004-2018 estimate must again be +0.0550 and its n 9,412). (4) If ARM 0 differs from the stored table, every difference is measured and attributed in QUERY 1 before the premium of ARM 0 or ARM 1 is read.

**Stated in advance.** (a) ARM 0 may differ from the stored table for three known reasons: the view event_component_dates (candidate_review_log changed on 2026-10-29), the date corrections of 2026-10-29 (the month and the volatility lookup of the corrected events), and events added since 2026-10-20. (b) ARM 1 is expected to be almost identical to ARM 0 (6 of 16,993 stored rows belong to excluded events). (c) The margin that decides p36: the lower end of the stored interval is 0.0073, that is 0.80 SE, above +0.0295; a downward shift of the pooled estimate of that size would move the category from B to A. (d) The standard errors are naive, as the rule says; the clustered SE is reported beside them, and if the clustered z of the pooled estimate falls below 2 this is recorded as weaker evidence while the registered verdict stays on the naive rule. (e) The ledger's own caveats stand: a current-index universe (survivorship) and weak older events mixed in. (f) The new tables and functions are additive; nothing existing is modified; they are listed for deletion after 2026-11-28. (g) Sealed versus non-sealed is not reported: the table has no company-set split. Nothing here re-scores or edits a prediction.

(further dated addenda go here: the remaining procedures)"""
head_before = T[:T.index("PRED-TABLE-END")]
R = T.replace(old, new)
assert R[:R.index("PRED-TABLE-END")] == head_before, "a registered prediction would change; nothing written"
open(p, "w", encoding="utf-8").write(R)
R2 = open(p, encoding="utf-8").read(); print("ok   Addendum 10 appended"); print("PREREG sha256", sha(R2), "|", R2.count("\n"), "lines")
